      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n7≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $1$\n- $2$\n- $10$",
      "worked_solution": "To visualize congruence modulo $3,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $3$ numbers. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114803/assets/46c64b99bf297e841b79bcc2e97c8a650b6ae94af8e11f2d4e85f2e8513def6c.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $3.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-1)÷3=6÷3=2\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $3∤(7-2).$ Notice that $7-2=5,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $3∣(7-10).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(7-10)÷3=-3÷3=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$1$ and $10$ only.\"",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
